{"rows": [
{"n": 1, "label": "requested", "why": "The request says \"`PUT /tickets/:id/assignee` with a JSON body `{\"assignee\": \"<agent>\"}`\" and \"`GET /tickets` gains an `assignee` filter\"; this row decides how those routes validate input."},
{"n": 2, "label": "requested", "why": "The request says \"The agent a ticket is assigned to is notified that it was assigned to them\"; using the outbox and a template only decides how that notification is sent."},
{"n": 3, "label": "implied", "why": "\"Every ticket shows its `assignee` (null when unassigned)\" fails unless the field is stored, and \"Everything the service already does keeps working\" requires the existing audit to keep working."},
{"n": 4, "label": "implied", "why": "Tickets already stored without the field would break \"Every ticket shows its `assignee` (null when unassigned)\" and existing behaviour unless they are read as null."},
{"n": 5, "label": "implied", "why": "Assignments that run at the same time must not corrupt tickets or break \"Assigning a ticket to the agent it already has changes nothing\"; the row reuses the existing synchronous ordering and adds nothing."},
{"n": 6, "label": "requested", "why": "The request says \"an agent is a non-empty name of at most 64 characters\"; the row enforces that rule and keeps the existing HTTP boundaries, adding no new access control."},
{"n": 7, "label": "implied", "why": "If assignment or notification fails, the failure must be visible to whoever has to act; reusing the existing audit and outbox reports covers this without adding a new channel."}
]}
